{"command": "cd /testbed && sed -n '1,40p' specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Tagged Cache (TaggedCache)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: cachetools milestone 1 - build a bounded, tag-aware cache (TaggedCache) with put/get/invalidate, deep-copy value isolation, tag membership lifecycle, LRU eviction, and strict input validation, while preserving all existing cachetools APIs.\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Store and Retrieve Tagged Entries (Priority: P1)\n\nA library user creates a bounded tagged cache and stores values under keys, optionally labeled with tags. Retrieval returns a fully detached deep copy of the stored value; mutating the returned copy or the original value never affects the cache, and values are copied on input. Missing keys raise KeyError. Invalid tags (a bare string, or a non-string entry inside the iterable) are rejected with ValueError without altering any existing cache state.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core value proposition - safe, isolated, tag-labeled storage. Without it, no other story is usable.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fully testable by creating the cache, putting values (plain, nested, with duplicate tags), getting them back, mutating copies, and asserting isolation, KeyError on missing keys, and ValueError with state unchanged on invalid tags.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty cache, **When** put(key, value, tags=(\"a\", \"b\")) is called, **Then** the entry is stored, len(cache) increases by 1, and get(key) returns a deep copy equal to the original value.\n2. **Given** a cached value that is a mutable structure, **When** the caller mutates the object returned by get(key) or the original object passed to put, **Then** subsequent get(key) returns the unmutated stored value (detached deep copy on output, copy on input).\n3. **Given** a cache containing key k, **When** get(missing) is called for an absent key, **Then** KeyError is raised and cache state is unchanged.\n4. **Given** a non-empty cache, **When** put is called with tags as a bare string (e.g. \"ab\") or with a non-string entry in the tag iterable, **Then** ValueError is raised and the previous entry for that key (value, tags, recency) plus all other state is unchanged.\n5. **Given** put(key, value, tags=(\"a\", \"a\", \"b\")), **When** the entry is later inspected via tag-based operations, **Then** duplicate tags are deduplicated (the entry carries tags {\"a\", \"b\"}).\n6. **Given** an unhashable key (e.g. a list), **When** put or get is attempted, **Then** the standard unhashable-key error (TypeError) is raised and state is unchanged.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by Tag and Manage Membership Lifecycle (Priority: P2)\n\nA library user removes all currently cached entries carrying a given tag in one call and learns how many were removed. Replacing an entry under an existing key drops its old tag memberships. Entries evicted by capacity limits leave no stale tag memberships that could affect later reuse of the same key. len(cache) always counts live entries.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based invalidation is the distinguishing capability over a plain LRU cache; it depends on P1 storage but is independently testable once entries exist.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fully testable by populating entries with overlapping tags, calling invalidate(tag), and asserting exact removal counts, surviving entries, and len(cache); plus a replace-then-invalidate and an evict-then-reuse sequence.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n"}